  "limits": [
    "Static display coverage from the same 2001 NASA composite as the native tiles; not simulated-date weather, cloud-water, radiance, RF loss, or a thermal input.",
    "Downsampling removes spatial detail. Blank source coverage, polar gaps, mosaic boundaries and other source artifacts remain; absent coverage is not filled.",
    "Matching the source prevents camera LOD changes from switching between different cloud composites. Native lossy WebP tiles and the area-reduced global map can still differ at the pixel and filtering scale.",
    "Original Solar System Scope cloud maps remain bundled as legacy assets with their original provenance and license.",
    "The checked western and eastern source images have a hard cloud-coverage discontinuity at longitude 0 degrees, visible over the Southern Ocean. This fixed geographical seam is preserved in both the global maps and native tiles; it is not a runtime tile boundary.",
    "The display-only soft toe attenuates genuine faint clouds as well as low-gray mosaic plateaus. Codes64..255 remain unchanged; R8 quantization and lossy native-tile compression remain. This is not calibrated cloud fraction, optical depth, gamma correction or weather; bright source discontinuities and the longitude0 seam are not repaired."
  ],
  "display_transfer": {
    "revision": "cloud-mask-soft-toe-v1",
    "purpose": "Display-only low-code suppression of visible NASA composite swath plateaus; not a calibrated cloud fraction, optical depth or gamma correction.",
    "toe_code": 64,
    "formula": "For source code c in[0,255], t=min(1,c/64); output=floor(c*t*t*(3-2*t)+0.5). Codes64..255 are unchanged.",
    "quantization": "8-bit R8/gray coverage, round-half-up in both Python and JavaScript. Codes0..9 map to0; genuine faint cloud signal is reduced along with source artifacts.",
    "filter_order": "Apply to native source/decoded tile texels before area reduction or GPU mip generation. Never apply the nonlinear toe to an already filtered coverage sample."
  }
